ltering out null addresses from PICKUP shipments, which is the right defensive approach even though all items in this scenario are DELIVERY orders with actual addresses.\n\nOne more thing to verify: the table parsing preserves whitespace within cells, so when the inventory column shows something like `WH1:PROD-A=5 PROD-B=3`, that space between products is maintained as part of the cell value. I'm verifying that cell values are properly trimmed while preserving internal spaces—so `WH1:PROD-A=5 PROD-B=3` keeps its internal spacing after trimming, which my parser needs to split products correctly. I'm also confident about handling longer inventory strings with semicolons and multiple warehouses. Let me double-check the consolidation scenario in Table 4 to make sure the items and inventory columns align properly.\n\nNow I'm walking through the parsing logic for a multi-warehouse inventory string: WH1 has PROD-A with 5 units, WH2 has both PROD-A with 3 and PROD-B with 3, and WH3 has PROD-B with 5. For an order needing one unit each of PROD-A and PROD-B, only WH2 can fulfill both items in a single shipment, so the optimal solution is one shipment from WH2.\n\nNow I need to implement the type converters for the WarehouseInventory class. I'm assuming it takes a nested map structure where the outer map keys are warehouse IDs, the inner maps key product IDs, and the values are StockLevel objects created through factory methods like `inStock(qty)`, `backordered()`, and `preOrder()`. I'll document this assumption in the converter's description since the actual API might differ.\n\nWriting type converter logic...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10229,"cache_read_input_tokens":40338,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10229,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id"